-- title:
--   Euclidean norm bounded by $\sqrt{d}$ times the maximum norm
-- statement:
--   Let $d \in \mathbb{N}$ and let $x \in \mathbb{R}^d$, regarded as a Euclidean (i.e. $\ell^2$) space with coordinates $x_0,\dots,x_{d-1}$. Write
--   $$\|x\|_\infty \;=\; \sup_{0 \le i < d} |x_i|$$
--   for the maximum norm and $\|x\|$ for the Euclidean norm. Then
--   $$\|x\| \;\le\; \sqrt{d}\,\|x\|_\infty .$$
--   There are no hypotheses: the inequality holds for every dimension $d$ and every vector $x$.
--
--   This is the elementary comparison of the two norms that lets cubes and balls be traded for one another. Since a cube of side $h$ is a ball of radius $h/2$ for $\|\cdot\|_\infty$, the estimate says such a cube is contained in a Euclidean ball of radius $\sqrt{d}\,h/2$, which is how cube-based discretizations are converted into statements about Euclidean distances.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** The maximum norm is defined as the supremum $\bigsqcup_i |x_i|$ over the finite index type $\mathrm{Fin}\,d$. In the degenerate case $d = 0$ the supremum over the empty index set is $0$ and both sides of the inequality vanish.
-- source:
